        "content": "     1→[\n     2→  {\n     3→    \"title\": \"Bryan Cantrill on LLMs and the Peril of Laziness Lost\",\n     4→    \"description\": \"Bryan Cantrill argues that LLMs inherently lack the virtue of laziness - work costs nothing to an LLM, so they don't optimize for future time or develop crisp abstractions. This highlights how essential human laziness is: our finite time forces us to develop good abstractions because we don't want to waste time on clunky ones.\",\n     5→    \"author\": \"Bryan Cantrill (quoted by Simon Willison)\",\n     6→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n     7→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/\",\n     8→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-13\",\n     9→    \"topics\": [\"ai-assisted-programming\", \"llms\", \"generative-ai\", \"software-engineering\"],\n    10→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n    11→  },\n    12→  {\n    13→    \"title\": \"Gemma 4 E2B Audio Transcription with MLX\",\n    14→    \"description\": \"Simon shared a recipe for transcribing audio files on macOS using the 10.28GB Gemma 4 E2B model with MLX and mlx-vlm. The command uses uv run with Python 3.13 and the mlx_vlm package to transcribe audio files effectively.\",\n    15→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    16→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    17→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/\",\n    18→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-12\",\n    19→    \"topics\": [\"gemma\", \"mlx\", \"speech-to-text\", \"python\", \"uv\", \"generative-ai\"],\n    20→    \"type\": \"tutorial\"\n    21→  },\n    22→  {\n    23→    \"title\": \"Andrej Karpathy: Growing Gap in AI Capability Understanding\",\n    24→    \"description\": \"Andrej Karpathy tweeted about the growing gap in understanding of AI capability based on access points and domains. OpenAI's free 'Advanced Voice Mode' runs on older GPT-4o era models and fumbles basic questions, while their highest-tier paid Codex model can coherently restructure entire codebases or find vulnerabilities. This works because coding domains offer explicit reward functions verifiable through unit tests and are more valuable in B2B settings.\",\n    25→    \"author\": \"Andrej Karpathy (covered by Simon Willison)\",\n    26→    \"source\": \"Twitter\",\n    27→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/\",\n    28→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-10\",\n    29→    \"topics\": [\"openai\", \"chatgpt\", \"llms\", \"ai-assisted-programming\", \"generative-ai\"],\n    30→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n    31→  },\n    32→  {\n    33→    \"title\": \"Karpathy's Shift from AI Coding to Second Brain\",\n    34→    \"description\": \"Andrej Karpathy reportedly stopped using AI to write code and is instead focusing on using LLMs to build a 'Second Brain' - a personal knowledge base system. He developed an LLM Knowledge Base architecture that bypasses traditional RAG, using an evolving markdown library maintained by AI with tools like Obsidian for capturing notes and web articles.\",\n    35→    \"author\": \"Andrej Karpathy\",\n    36→    \"source\": \"Tech Media\",\n    37→    \"url\": \"https://medium.com/neuralnotions/andrej-karpathy-stopped-using-ai-to-write-code-hes-using-it-to-build-a-second-brain-instead-cddceadc5df5\",\n    38→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-11\",\n    39→    \"topics\": [\"llm\", \"knowledge-base\", \"personal-ai\", \"obsidian\", \"rag\"],\n    40→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n    41→  },\n    42→  {\n    43→    \"title\": \"Karpathy's 'AI Psychosis' Warning for Developers\",\n    44→    \"description\": \"Andrej Karpathy warned about 'AI psychosis' among developers, saying he 'started to lose his ability to code' due to over-reliance on AI programming tools. He predicted 2026 would be the 'Slopacolypse' and mentioned that 'AI is suddenly writing most of his code' causing him to 'start to atrophy his ability to write code.' By early 2026, 51% of all code committed to GitHub was generated or substantially assisted by AI.\",\n    45→    \"author\": \"Andrej Karpathy\",\n    46→    \"source\": \"Tech Media\",\n    47→    \"url\": \"https://thenewstack.io/karpathy-says-developers-have-ai-psychosis-everyone-else-is-next/\",\n    48→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-11\",\n    49→    \"topics\": [\"ai-assisted-programming\", \"developer-tools\", \"code-atrophy\", \"vibe-coding\"],\n    50→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n    51→  },\n    52→  {\n    53→    \"title\": \"Meta's Muse Spark Model Launch with Interesting Tool Capabilities\",\n    54→    \"description\": \"Meta announced Muse Spark, their first model release since Llama 4 almost exactly a year ago. It's hosted (not open weights) and currently in private API preview, but available on meta.ai with Facebook or Instagram login. The model features interesting tool capabilities and reasoning features.\",\n    55→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    56→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    57→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/\",\n    58→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-08\",\n    59→    \"topics\": [\"llm-release\", \"meta\", \"llm-tool-use\", \"llm-reasoning\", \"code-interpreter\"],\n    60→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    61→  },\n    62→  {\n    63→    \"title\": \"Anthropic's Project Glasswing - Restricting Claude Mythos to Security Researchers\",\n    64→    \"description\": \"Anthropic didn't release their latest model, Claude Mythos, to the public. Instead, they made it available to a very restricted set of preview partners under Project Glasswing. Simon discusses why this sounds necessary given the security implications of powerful AI models.\",\n    65→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    66→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    67→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/\",\n    68→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-07\",\n    69→    \"topics\": [\"anthropic\", \"llm-release\", \"ai-security-research\", \"ai-ethics\", \"security\"],\n    70→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n    71→  },\n    72→  {\n    73→    \"title\": \"GLM-5.1: 754B Parameter Model with Advanced Code Generation\",\n    74→    \"description\": \"Chinese AI lab Z.ai's GLM-5.1 is a 754B parameter, 1.51TB MIT-licensed model. Simon tested its ability to generate SVG with CSS animations, including fixing broken animations when pointed out. The model demonstrated sophisticated understanding of SVG, CSS transforms, and animation issues.\",\n    75→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    76→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    77→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/\",\n    78→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-07\",\n    79→    \"topics\": [\"llm-release\", \"glm\", \"ai-in-china\", \"svg\", \"css\", \"generative-ai\"],\n    80→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    81→  },\n    82→  {\n    83→    \"title\": \"Google AI Edge Gallery: Local Gemma 4 on iPhone\",\n    84→    \"description\": \"Google released AI Edge Gallery, an official app for running Gemma 4 models (E2B and E4B sizes) directly on iPhone. The 2.54GB E2B model is fast and useful. The app includes image questions, audio transcription (up to 30s), and interesting 'skills' demos showing tool calling against eight different interactive widgets implemented as HTML pages.\",\n    85→    \"author\": \"Simon Willison\",\n    86→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    87→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/\",\n    88→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-06\",\n    89→    \"topics\": [\"google\", \"local-llms\", \"gemma\", \"iphone\", \"llm-tool-use\"],\n    90→    \"type\": \"technical\"\n    91→  },\n    92→  {\n    93→    \"title\": \"Eight Years of Wanting, Three Months of Building with AI\",\n    94→    \"description\": \"Lalit Maganti's deep dive into building syntaqlite, high-fidelity devtools for SQLite. The project took 8 years of thinking but only 3 months of building, thanks to AI. Key insights: AI excels at tedious work (400+ grammar rules) but can cause procrastination on design decisions. The first vibe-coded prototype worked as proof of concept but had poor architecture, requiring a complete restart with more human decision-making. AI struggles with tasks that have no objectively checkable answer like design.\",\n    95→    \"author\": \"Lalit Maganti (covered by Simon Willison)\",\n    96→    \"source\": \"Blog\",\n    97→    \"url\": \"https://simonwillison.net/\",\n    98→    \"published_date\": \"2026-04-05\",\n    99→    \"topics\": [\"sqlite\", \"ai-assisted-programming\", \"vibe-coding\", \"agentic-engineering\", \"software-design\"],\n   100→    \"type\": \"opinion\"\n   101→  }\n   102→]\n   103→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"
      }
    ]
